+What you can expect a condo to earn and cost here, from about 2,600 condo sales, 25 years of home values and a year of rental data.
 
-Decider one
+The short answer
+Only if it rents like the busier listings here. The HOA fee and tax on a typical oceanfront two-bedroom come to about $18,000 a year. A rental-data company estimates that the average nightly rental in the Myrtle Beach market keeps about $16,000 after its running costs, so it falls short. A rental at the top-quarter mark for income, rented all year, keeps about $28,000.
+As a bet on the price, a house has done better. Since 2001 the typical condo here has about doubled in value, and the typical house has grown about 2.5 times.
 
-## The building matters more than the unit
-Two identical units a block apart can earn different amounts because the buildings differ: rental history, HOA dues, the master insurance policy, reserve savings, and upcoming special assessments. The right building can produce far more revenue than a nearly identical unit in the wrong one. We keep a building-by-building map of operations and fees: the oceanfront building directory .
-Before any offer, we read the building's bylaws, budget, and reserve study, and we ask for its rental history. A pretty unit in a struggling building is still a bad investment.
