+## Can you rent out a Myrtle Beach condo by the night?
+In most oceanfront buildings, yes. About 94 in 100 condos for sale in oceanfront buildings say they allow short-term rentals. Across all condos for sale in the county it is about 7 in 10.
+Inside the City of Myrtle Beach, nightly rentals are legal only in the zones the city shades on its rental map , apart from 24 grandfathered houses. They are most of the oceanfront hotel and condo strip, the Broadway at the Beach area, stretches of the US 17 Bypass and US 501, and The Market Common's town center. About 3 in 4 condos in the city sit in those zones.
+Some buildings ban renting at all. We filter those out of a search and read the HOA papers for any rule that would block your rental plan.
+
+### Who are your neighbors in a condo building?
+Mostly investors and second-home owners. About 1 in 5 condo units here is taxed at the 4 percent rate for a home its owner lives in. Of the county's other homes, about 6 in 10 are.
+Homes in Horry County taxed at the 4 percent rate for a home its owner lives in Kind of home share
+
+Condo units About 1 in 5
+Every other kind of home About 6 in 10
+
+County parcel records. The rest are taxed at 6 percent: rentals, second homes and homes whose owner has not claimed the lower rate.
+Most of your neighbors will rent their units out or use them part of the year.
+
+## Can you get a mortgage on a Myrtle Beach oceanfront condo?
+Often not a normal one. Fannie Mae, one of the two big companies that buy normal home loans from lenders, will not buy a loan on a unit in a building run like a hotel , even if each unit is owned separately. The signs it lists:
+
+- registration services that check guests in, as a hotel does
+
+- the HOA or its manager renting units by the night or for short stays
+
+- daily cleaning
+
+- one key system for every unit, run by the building
+
+- owners required to put their units in a shared rental pool
+
+- rent shared with the HOA or a rental company
+
+It also will not buy one where one person or company owns too many of the units, or in a building facing large repairs that no one has paid for.
+
+### What is a non-warrantable condo?
+A condo in a building that fails these tests. Lenders call it non-warrantable . Buildings with a rental program usually are, because so many owners are investors. That also shows the building is run for renting.
+For you it means a larger down payment, so more of your cash stays in that unit and cannot fund the next purchase. A loan officer at our preferred lender can tell you which loans work, including a DSCR loan , which is approved on what the unit rents for.
+
+### Can you use an FHA or VA loan on a Myrtle Beach condo?
+Rarely. About 1 in 9 condos for sale say they accept an FHA loan, and about 1 in 12 a VA loan. That is what the seller's agent wrote. The FHA's own list holds only 9 approved condo buildings in Horry County, so we confirm the building with the lender before you offer.
+
+## Where are the bargains on Myrtle Beach condos?
+With an owner in trouble, in a sound building. In our own files, condos bought from a distressed owner in a stable building sold for about 22 percent less on average. The building still qualifies for normal loans; only that one unit was mismanaged.
+Want a condo like that?
+Tell us your price range. An agent looks for condos sold by an owner in trouble, in buildings that still qualify for normal loans.
+Have us find you your next investment
+
+## What should you look for in a Myrtle Beach condo building?
+The building matters more than the unit. Two units with the same view can cost and earn different amounts because their buildings differ. Five parts of a building matter most:
+
+- The budget and the fee. What the fee pays for, how much it has risen, and whether the HOA collects enough to pay its bills.
+
+- The savings for repairs. How much the HOA has saved for roofs, elevators and balconies, and any special assessment voted or planned. An older building may charge one for a large repair.
+
+- The rental rules. Whether the rules allow short-term rentals, and whether the building runs its own rental program.
+
+- The loan. Whether a normal loan, an FHA loan or a VA loan works in the building.
+
+- The flood zone. About 1 in 6 condos sold last year sat in a high-risk flood zone, against about 1 in 10 other homes.
+
+### Can you owe the seller's HOA dues?
+Yes, up to a limit. Under South Carolina's condo law, a buyer owes the seller's unpaid HOA dues together with the seller, unless the buyer bought at a foreclosure sale. Your share is capped at the amount on a statement the HOA must give you when you ask for it, so the amount is known before closing.
 
 Common questions
 
-## Frequently asked questions
+## Myrtle Beach condo investing FAQ
 
-### Are Myrtle Beach condos a good investment?
-They can be. Prices start lower than almost any coastal market and vacation demand is deep, with roughly 19 million visitors a year. The outcome depends on the building's finances and rental history, the loan you can get on it, and the management deal. Judge those three, not the countertops.
+### Can you live in a Myrtle Beach condo and rent it out part of the year?
+Yes, but the home you live in loses the 4 percent tax rate if it is rented out more than 72 days in a calendar year, unless its owner is on active military duty under orders. Then it is taxed at 6 percent, like any rental.
 
-### Do I have to use my condo building's on-site property manager?
-Usually no, but some buildings write requirements or restrictions into the master deed, so check it before you buy. Many of the strongest buildings have the most expensive on-site managers, and owners often earn more after comparing prices and results with other local property management companies.
+### What do you pay the HOA when you buy a Myrtle Beach condo?
+Usually a transfer fee, and often a few months of dues paid ahead into escrow. Both are common in condo buildings here, with or without a rental program.
 
-### Why is condo financing different in Myrtle Beach?
-Many oceanfront buildings are condotels, which most conventional lenders will not finance. Those buildings need specialty loans, larger down payments, or DSCR loans that qualify on the rent. Confirm the building's status with a lender before you make an offer.
+### Is it harder to sell a Myrtle Beach condo than a house?
