ottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.05)">Jan</td><td style="padding:.3rem .5rem;text-align:right;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.05)">23%</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.3rem .5rem;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.05)">Feb</td><td style="padding:.3rem .5rem;text-align:right;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.05)">29%</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.3rem .5rem;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.05)">Mar</td><td style="padding:.3rem .5rem;text-align:right;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.05)">42%</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.3rem .5rem;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.05)">Apr</td><td style="padding:.3rem .5rem;text-align:right;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.05)">59%</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.3rem .5rem;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.05)">May</td><td style="padding:.3rem .5rem;text-align:right;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.05)">66%</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.3rem .5rem;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.05)">Jun</td><td style="padding:.3rem .5rem;text-align:right;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.05)">81%</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.3rem .5rem;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.05)">Jul</td><td style="padding:.3rem .5rem;text-align:right;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.05)">88%</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.3rem .5rem;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.05)">Aug</td><td style="padding:.3rem .5rem;text-align:right;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.05)">79%</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.3rem .5rem;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.05)">Sep</td><td style="padding:.3rem .5rem;text-align:right;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.05)">61%</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.3rem .5rem;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.05)">Oct</td><td style="padding:.3rem .5rem;text-align:right;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.05)">56%</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.3rem .5rem;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.05)">Nov</td><td style="padding:.3rem .5rem;text-align:right;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.05)">33%</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.3rem .5rem;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.05)">Dec</td><td style="padding:.3rem .5rem;text-align:right;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.05)">23%</td></tr></tbody></table><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.74rem;color:#6d675d;line-height:1.5;margin-top:.5rem">Illustrative seasonal pattern for Grand Strand short-term rentals. North Myrtle Beach often holds shoulder seasons slightly better thanks to golf and festival demand.</p></figure><div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid var(--rule);border-radius:8px;padding:1.5rem 1.6rem;margin:2rem 0"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.62rem;letter-spacing:.18em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;margin-bottom:1rem">Short-term rental data &middot; North Myrtle Beach</p><div style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(112px,1fr));gap:1.2rem 1rem;margin-bottom:1.15rem"><div><div style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.55rem;color:var(--navy);line-height:1.1">$28,667</div><div style="font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.05em;color:var(--muted);margin-top:.15rem">average annual revenue</div></div><div><div style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.55rem;color:var(--navy);line-height:1.1">32.2%</div><div style="font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.05em;color:var(--muted);margin-top:.15rem">occupancy</div></div><div><div style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.55rem;color:var(--navy);line-height:1.1">$374</div><div style="font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.05em;color:var(--muted);margin-top:.15rem">average nightly rate</div></div><div><div style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.55rem;color:var(--navy);line-height:1.1">4,406</div><div style="font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.05em;color:var(--muted);margin-top:.15rem">active listings</div></div></div><p style="color:var(--muted);font-size:.87rem;line-height:1.65;margin:0">The strongest tenth of listings here gross $8,753 a month or more; the median listing makes $2,729. June is the strongest month and January the weakest. Averages cover every listing in the market, including part-time ones, so a well-run property can sit well above them. <a href="/invest/airbnb-income/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">Compare all nine submarkets</a>. <span style="font-size:.78rem">AirROI, data through 2026-08-08.</span></p></div><h2>How rental returns work in North Myrtle Beach</h2><p>Returns on the larger beach houses are a revenue-per-booking story. A single group rental can cover what several condo bookings would, but the expenses run high: professional cleaning of a large home, landscaping, pool service where applicable, and management. Run the full-year net, not the summer gross, and weigh peak weekly rates against the slower months. The houses that clear the best returns are the ones positioned for big families and reviewed well enough to earn repeat bookings.</p><h2>Financing an investment property in North Myrtle Beach</h2><p>Single-family beach houses generally finance more conventionally than condotels, which is part of their appeal, but second-home and investment-property loans still carry higher down payments and rates than a primary residence. DSCR financing, which qualifies on the property's rental income rather than your personal income, is a common path for investors here. Line up the loan type before you shop so your offer is credible.</p><h2>North Myrtle Beach rules and costs that move the numbers</h2><p>Budget for the rules and the taxes as well as the mortgage. South Carolina taxes non-owner-occupied property at a 6 percent assessment ratio versus 4 percent for a primary residence, so an investment beach house carries a heavier tax line. Confirm the current rate for the parcel. Short-term rentals also remit state and local accommodations taxes and need proper licensing. On top of that, oceanfront and Cherry Grove channel homes sit in flood zones, so a real flood-insurance quote belongs in your numbers before you fall for the view.</p><h2>The North Myrtle Beach investor thesis</h2><ol class="thesis-list"><li><span class="thesis-num">01</span><span class="thesis-text">Larger beach houses positioned for family groups can outproduce condos on peak-season revenue per booking.</span></li><li><span class="thesis-num">02</span><span class="thesis-text">Cherry Grove channel homes are a defensible niche: dock access draws renters and buyers that beach-block inventory cannot.</span></li><li><span class="thesis-num">03</span><span class="thesis-text">Repeat, loyalty-driven demand makes revenue here steadier and easier to forecast than the high-churn strip.</span></li><li><span class="thesis-num">04</span><span class="thesis-text">Golf and shag-festival demand lengthens the rental calendar into spring and fall.</span></li></ol><h2>What a first North Myrtle Beach deal looks like</h2><p>A common first move here is a three or four-bedroom beach house positioned for family groups, bought with a clear-eyed view of the full-year calendar. Estimate the slow months as well as peak summer, confirm the community allows the rental frequency you need, get the elevation certificate and a real flood quote, and price in professional cleaning and management for a larger home. A well-reviewed house that sleeps a big group can outproduce two condos, but only when the operating costs were in the model from the start.</p><h2>What to watch before you buy in North Myrtle Beach</h2><ul style="padding-left:1.1rem;margin-top:.5rem"><li style="margin-bottom:.7rem;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7"><strong>Entry price on houses.</strong> The big beach houses cost more up front, so peak revenue has to carry more property. Estimate the full year, including the slow months.</li><li style="margin-bottom:.7rem;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7"><strong>Flood and elevation.</strong> Cherry Grove and oceanfront blocks sit in flood zones. Get the elevation certificate and a real insurance quote before you commit.</li><li style="margin-bottom:.7rem;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7"><strong>HOA and community rules.</strong> Some communities restrict rentals or short minimum stays. Confirm per address.</li></ul><h2>Where North Myrtle Beach sits, and how it is governed</h2><p>North Myrtle Beach is an incorporated Horry County city of roughly 17,000 to 18,000 residents, formed in 1968 by merging four beach communities that people still use as neighborhood names: Cherry Grove, Ocean Drive, Crescent Beach, and Windy Hill. Because it is its own city, it licenses its own rentals and collects its own accommodations fee.</p><h2>The North Myrtle Beach market right now</h2><p>Median sale prices were roughly $399,000 to $415,000 in early 2026 per Redfin and Zillow, trending a few percent below the 2025 peak. The internal spread is wide: two-bedroom condos start in the $200,000s and around a $268,000 median, while single-family homes carry a much higher median (well into the $600,000s), and Cherry Grove oceanfront and canal homes routinely exceed $1 million. Treat the headline median as a blend and price by segment and section. <!-- TIMMY: current CCAR MLS median and DOM for NMB by section (Cherry Grove, Ocean Drive, Crescent, Windy Hill, Barefoot). --></p><h2>Short-term rental rules in North Myrtle Beach</h2><p>North Myrtle Beach allows short-term rentals. Operators need a city business license, renewed every year. The application asks for an emergency contact and the number of parking spaces at the property. That number is the most guest cars allowed, and it must be posted in the unit and in every ad and rental agreement. The owner also collects and pays the accommodations taxes. The city does not require a rental permit, an inspection or a local agent. City zoning does not generally limit where rentals can operate, but an HOA, a planned development or a development agreement can add its own limits. See the <a href="https://library.municode.com/sc/north_myrtle_beach" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">North Myrtle Beach code of ordinances</a>. <!-- TIMMY: which NMB sections/buildings are strongest for STR returns. --> Every jurisdiction's licenses and taxes: <a href="/invest/str-rules/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">short-term rental rules by city</a>.</p><h2>Owning in North Myrtle Beach: the 4 percent versus 6 percent property tax rule</h2><p>South Carolina assesses an owner-occupied primary residence at a 4 percent ratio, and it does not pay the school operating tax. A second home or investment is assessed at 6 percent and pays the school operating tax. In North Myrtle Beach that makes a second home's tax bill about 3 times a primary home's on the same house. See the <a href="https://dor.sc.gov" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">South Carolina Department of Revenue</a> for the assessment-ratio rules. Full county math, with a calculator: <a href="/buyers/property-taxes/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">Horry County property taxes</a>.</p><h2>Flood zones and insurance in North Myrtle Beach</h2><p>Flood risk is a core cost factor on the North Strand. Public First Street data reported by Redfin flags a large share of North Myrtle Beach properties (around 44 percent) as facing elevated flood risk over a 30-year horizon, and Cherry Grove's canals and marsh edges carry particular exposure. Pull the FEMA flood map and, on low-lying or oceanfront lots, an elevation certificate before you commit. <!-- TIMMY: which streets/sections flood, and current flood and wind premium ranges. --> What the three coastal policies cost: <a href="/buyers/coastal-insurance/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">coastal home insurance costs</a>.</p><h2>North Myrtle Beach lifestyle and amenities</h2><p>North Myrtle Beach has a distinct identity from Myrtle Beach. Ocean Drive is the home of the shag (the state dance) and a walkable Main Street. Cherry Grove offers a fishing pier and a wide tidal marsh. Barefoot Resort has four championship golf courses and a marina on the Intracoastal. McLeod Seacoast provides local hospital care, and the overall feel is more residential and family-oriented than the entertainment core to the south.</p><h2>Schools serving North Myrtle Beach</h2><p>Public schools fall within the North Myrtle Beach attendance area of <a href="https://www.horrycountyschools.net/about-hcs/our-schools" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Horry County Schools</a>, including Ocean Drive Elementary, North Myrtle Beach Middle, and North Myrtle Beach High. Assignment is by address, and Chapter3 will confirm the exact schools for any property against the district attendance-boundary map.</p><p>Chapter3 does not rate schools as good or bad; you decide that from the full record. The honest way to research a school is to read the official, neutral sources directly. The <a href="https://screportcards.ed.sc.gov/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">South Carolina Department of Education school report cards</a> publish state accountability ratings, test performance, and graduation data for every public school. <a href="https://www.greatschools.org/south-carolina/north-myrtle-beach/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GreatSchools</a> aggregates ratings and parent reviews. Read both, visit the school, and talk to families in the specific attendance zone. The report card also publishes SAT and ACT averages, Advanced Placement and dual-enrollment participation, college-and-career readiness, dropout rate, and chronic absenteeism. Ask your Chapter3 agent and we will pull the report card and enrollment details for any address alongside you.</p><h2>Buying in North Myrtle Beach as a primary residence</h2><p>Most of what is written about North Myrtle Beach is aimed at investors, but a large share of buyers here want a primary residence or a second home, and the questions are different. If you are buying to live, weigh the year-round feel of the section you choose, golf-cart access, proximity to McLeod Seacoast for healthcare, and how seasonal the street gets in summer. Ocean Drive has a walkable Main Street and shag-dancing culture. Cherry Grove is quieter and marsh-facing. Condo buyers should scrutinize the regime (HOA) fees and the reserve study, because coastal insurance has pushed dues and special assessments up across the Strand.</p><p>The primary-versus-second-home decision also changes your tax bill directly. A primary residence is assessed at South Carolina's 4 percent ratio and does not pay the school operating tax. A second home is assessed at 6 percent and pays it. Budget for the higher effective rate if this will not be your full-time home. For a live-in buyer, resale, walkability, flood elevation, and HOA rules usually matter more than nightly-rate projections, and we weight the search accordingly.</p><h2>Read the North Myrtle Beach HOA rules before you commit to a house</h2><p>In a market this HOA-heavy, the covenants matter as much as the house. Before you make an offer, read the association's bylaws and rules, because they can restrict short-term and long-term rentals, pets, parking, golf carts, fences, exterior changes, and more. The classic trap: you have spent years training your dog to walk off leash, then buy into a community with a strict leash rule and lose the thing you valued most. That is a fixable mistake if you find it before closing and an expensive one if you do not.</p><p>Ask us and we will get you the governing documents for any community, from our own files if we already have them or by requesting them for you if we do not. Then you read the rules themselves rather than a listing summary. It is also why, when you call a Chapter3 agent, we ask a lot of questions about you and how you live. We would rather spend the extra time up front than match you to a home with a rule or an HOA that blocks what you enjoy or turns out to be a poor investment.</p><h2>What it is like to live in North Myrtle Beach</h2><p>For a primary residence or a full-time second home, the day-to-day here is quieter and more settled than the Myrtle Beach core. Ocean Drive has an actual walkable Main Street and is the historic home of the shag, South Carolina's state dance, so there is a real downtown to live near rather than just a strip of rentals. Cherry Grove, at the north end, is calmer and marsh-facing, with a fishing pier and tidal creeks instead of nightlife. Golf carts are a normal way to get around; South Carolina allows registered carts on secondary roads in daylight within a few miles of home.</p><p>Everyday life is well covered: grocery and retail along Highway 17, four championship courses and a marina at Barefoot Resort, and McLeod Seacoast for hospital care. Commutes are short for residents who work locally, and the community has a year-round feel outside the summer peak. As a live-in buyer, three things matter more than nightly-rate projections. Check the flood elevation and insurance cost for the exact lot. On a condo, check the regime or HOA dues and the reserve study. Read the community rules covered below. Those are the numbers that shape daily life here.</p><h2>North Myrtle Beach community stats</h2><p>Official figures from government sources, with no interpretation added. The links go to the source so you can read the full data yourself.</p><div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid var(--rule);border-radius:12px;padding:1.25rem 1.4rem;margin:1rem 0"><p style="font-size:.72rem;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;margin-bottom:.5rem">Population &amp; income &middot; <a href="https://www.census.gov/quickfacts/fact/table/northmyrtlebeachcitysouthcarolina" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">U.S. Census Bureau</a></p><div style="display:flex;justify-content:space-between;gap:1rem;padding:.55rem 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.06)"><span style="color:var(--navy)">Population (2020 Census)</span><span style="color:var(--muted);text-align:right">18,790</span></div><div style="display:flex;justify-content:space-between;gap:1rem;padding:.55rem 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.06)"><span style="color:var(--navy)">Population (2000 Census)</span><span style="color:var(--muted);text-align:right">10,974</span></div><div style="display:flex;justify-content:space-between;gap:1rem;padding:.55rem 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.06)"><span style="color:var(--navy)">Median household income (ACS)</span><span style="color:var(--muted);text-align:right">$72,722</span></div><div style="display:flex;justify-content:space-between;gap:1rem;padding:.55rem 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.06)"><span style="color:var(--navy)">Persons in poverty (ACS)</span><span style="color:var(--muted);text-align:right">7.4%</span></div><p style="font-size:.72rem;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;margin:1.25rem 0 .5rem">Cost, taxes &amp; risk &middot; government sources</p><div style="display:flex;justify-content:space-between;gap:1rem;padding:.55rem 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.06)"><span style="color:var(--navy)">Median owner-occupied home value (ACS)</span><span style="color:var(--muted);text-align:right">about $452,000 &nbsp;<a href="https://www.census.gov/quickfacts/fact/table/northmyrtlebeachcitysouthcarolina" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Census</a></span></div><div style="display:flex;justify-content:space-between;gap:1rem;padding:.55rem 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.06)"><span style="color:var(--navy)">Property assessment ratio</span><span style="color:var(--muted);text-align:right">4% primary / 6% second home &nbsp;<a href="https://dor.sc.gov" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC DOR</a></span></div><div style="display:flex;justify-content:space-between;gap:1rem;padding:.55rem 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.06)"><span style="color:var(--navy)">Flood hazard by address</span><span style="color:var(--muted);text-align:right"><a href="https://msc.fema.gov" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">FEMA Flood Map Service Center</a></span></div><div style="display:flex;justify-content:space-between;gap:1rem;padding:.55rem 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.06)"><span style="color:var(--navy)">Crime statistics</span><span style="color:var(--muted);text-align:right"><a href="https://cde.ucr.cjis.gov" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">FBI Crime Data Explorer</a> &middot; <a href="https://www.sled.sc.gov" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SLED</a></span></div><div style="display:flex;justify-content:space-between;gap:1rem;padding:.55rem 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.06)"><span style="color:var(--navy)">School performance</span><span style="color:var(--muted);text-align:right"><a href="https://screportcards.ed.sc.gov" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC Dept. of Education report cards</a></span></div></div><p style="color:var(--muted);font-size:.85rem;line-height:1.7">How to pull the crime numbers yourself: on the <a href="https://cde.ucr.cjis.gov" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">FBI Crime Data Explorer</a>, choose South Carolina, then select the local agency to see reported offenses by year. The local agency is the county sheriff's office or the city police department. On <a href="https://www.sled.sc.gov" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SLED</a>, open the current "Crime in South Carolina" report and find the county and agency tables. Chapter3 will pull the current figures for a specific address with you.</p><h2>North Myrtle Beach school stats</h2><p>North Myrtle Beach High School serves the North Myrtle Beach attendance area within Horry County Schools. Figures below are from the South Carolina Department of Education report card and federal education data, with no interpretation added.</p><div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid var(--rule);border-radius:12px;padding:1.25rem 1.4rem;margin:1rem 0"><p style="font-size:.72rem;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;margin-bottom:.5rem">North Myrtle Beach High School &middot; <a href="https://screportcards.ed.sc.gov" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC report card</a></p><div style="display:flex;justify-content:space-between;gap:1rem;padding:.55rem 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.06)"><span style="color:var(--navy)">State report card rating</span><span style="color:var(--muted);text-align:right">Substantially exceeds expectations</span></div><div style="display:flex;justify-content:space-between;gap:1rem;padding:.55rem 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.06)"><span style="color:var(--navy)">Grades served</span><span style="color:var(--muted);text-align:right">9-12</span></div><div style="display:flex;justify-content:space-between;gap:1rem;padding:.55rem 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.06)"><span style="color:var(--navy)">Enrollment</span><span style="color:var(--muted);text-align:right">about 1,491</span></div><div style="display:flex;justify-content:space-between;gap:1rem;padding:.55rem 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.06)"><span style="color:var(--navy)">Four-year graduation rate</span><span style="color:var(--muted);text-align:right">about 83% (state average about 84%)</span></div><div style="display:flex;justify-content:space-between;gap:1rem;padding:.55rem 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.06)"><span style="color:var(--navy)">Math proficiency</span><span style="color:var(--muted);text-align:right">71% (state average 41%)</span></div><div style="display:flex;justify-content:space-between;gap:1rem;padding:.55rem 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.06)"><span style="color:var(--navy)">Reading and writing proficiency</span><span style="color:var(--muted);text-align:right">89% (state average 52%)</span></div><div style="display:flex;justify-content:space-between;gap:1rem;padding:.55rem 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.06)"><span style="color:var(--navy)">Student-to-teacher ratio</span><span style="color:var(--muted);text-align:right">18 to 1</span></div></div><p>For the elementary and middle schools that feed it, and for any exact address, read the full report cards at the <a href="https://screportcards.ed.sc.gov" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">South Carolina Department of Education</a>. Ask your Chapter3 agent and we will pull the report card for the specific school an address is zoned to.</p><h2>Who North Myrtle Beach suits</h2><p>North Myrtle Beach fits STR investors who keep a city license current, second-home owners who want a quieter beach than the Myrtle Beach core, and golfers drawn to Barefoot and the surrounding courses. The city enforces its parking, trash, noise and party rules on rentals. If you want an established, amenity-rich beach town, this is a strong fit.</p></div>
+        <div class="detail-main"><h2>Real estate investing in North Myrtle Beach, SC</h2><p>North Myrtle Beach is the Grand Strand's family-and-golf beach. Made up of the historic sections of Cherry Grove, Ocean Drive, Crescent Beach, and Windy Hill, it draws repeat vacationers who return to the same streets year after year. That repeat demand gives the short-term rental market a steadier, more loyal base than the high-churn Myrtle Beach strip, and it supports a higher-end inventory of beach houses alongside the condos.</p><p>The city is its own municipality with its own character. It is quieter and more residential than Myrtle Beach, and leans toward families and golfers rather than the boardwalk crowd. It includes the Ocean Drive section, the center of the region's shag-dancing culture and its spring and fall SOS festivals. Those festivals extend the rental calendar into the shoulder seasons.</p><h2>North Myrtle Beach property types and pricing</h2><p>North Myrtle Beach offers a mixed inventory: oceanfront and second-row condos, channel and canal homes in Cherry Grove with private docks, and larger single-family beach houses. Those beach houses sleep ten or more and command strong weekly rates in summer. The larger homes carry higher entry prices but can produce outsized peak-season revenue when they are positioned for big family groups.</p><p>Cherry Grove's tidal channels are a distinct niche. Homes on the channels offer boat and fishing access that pure beach blocks do not, and that feature carries its own demand from a specific buyer and renter. Pricing across the city runs from entry condos into the multi-million-dollar range for prime oceanfront houses.</p><h2>Where investors are looking in North Myrtle Beach</h2><p>The city's four historic sections each behave a little differently. Cherry Grove draws investors to its tidal channels, where dock access commands a premium. Ocean Drive is the walkable, festival-anchored core with steady repeat demand. Crescent Beach and Windy Hill round out the oceanfront with a mix of condos and houses. Just inland, Barefoot Resort along the Intracoastal Waterway adds golf-community and waterway inventory that broadens the buyer pool beyond pure beach product.</p><h2>The North Myrtle Beach rental market</h2><p>Short-term rental is well established and broadly accepted here, which is part of the appeal: it is a purpose-built vacation market rather than a residential one fighting over rental rights. The larger beach houses in particular benefit from group travel, where a single booking covers what several condo bookings would. Long-term rental exists but is secondary to vacation rental.</p><p>As everywhere on the Strand, the specifics still matter. HOA rules vary building to building and community to community, and the city administers business licensing and accommodations tax on short-term rentals. The seasonal revenue curve mirrors the regional pattern, with the shag festivals adding useful shoulder-season pop that the pure-summer markets lack.</p><figure style="margin:2rem 0;background:#fff;border:1px solid var(--rule);border-radius:12px;padding:1.5rem 1.25rem 1rem"><figcaption style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.72rem;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;margin-bottom:1rem">Typical short-term rental occupancy by month</figcaption><svg viewBox="0 0 680 320" style="width:100%;height:auto" role="img" aria-label="Bar chart of typical short-term rental occupancy by month: Jan 23 percent, Feb 29 percent, Mar 42 percent, Apr 59 percent, May 66 percent, Jun 81 percent, Jul 88 percent, Aug 79 percent, Sep 61 percent, Oct 56 percent, Nov 33 percent, Dec 23 percent."><style>.bar rect{transition:fill .15s ease}.bar:hover rect{fill:#a86230}</style><line x1="56" y1="268.0" x2="664" y2="268.0" stroke="rgba(28,32,40,.08)" stroke-width="1"/><text x="48" y="271.0" font-family="DM Sans,sans-serif" font-size="10" fill="#8a8276" text-anchor="end">0%</text><li